# On the server: Caddy terminates HTTPS for LODGIVA_DOMAIN (a real domain,
# or <ip-with-dashes>.sslip.io for a free one) and certificates are issued
# and renewed automatically by Let's Encrypt. setup.sh adds a global
# `email` block above this only when ACME_EMAIL is given.

import Caddyfile.routes

{$LODGIVA_DOMAIN} {
	encode zstd gzip
	import lodgiva_routes
}

# One Frappe site per hotel customer, reached at <customer>.<apex>.
#
# Certificates are issued on first request rather than from one wildcard
# certificate, which keeps this on the stock caddy:2 image: a wildcard needs a
# DNS-01 challenge, and that needs a Caddy built with a DNS provider module
# plus an API token for the zone.
#
# The cost of on-demand issuance is that any hostname resolving here would
# otherwise trigger a certificate request, so the `ask` endpoint in the global
# block (written by setup.sh) decides which hosts are real. Creating a customer
# therefore needs no change here and no reload.
*.{$LODGIVA_APEX} {
	encode zstd gzip
	tls {
		on_demand
	}
	reverse_proxy {$FRAPPE_UPSTREAM}
}
